'''Byron Lowell Donalds''' (born October 28, 1978) is an American politician serving as the [[U.S. representative]] for [[Florida's 19th congressional district]] since 2021. His congressional district includes much of [[Southwest Florida]]. From 2016 to 2020, he served in the [[Florida House of Representatives]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], Donalds is its nominee in the [[2026 Florida gubernatorial election]].

Born and raised in [[Crown Heights, Brooklyn]], Donalds attended [[Florida A&M University]] before earning a Bachelor of Science degree in finance and marketing from [[Florida State University]] in 2002. Before entering politics, he worked in the finance, insurance, and banking industries. Situated in the [[Factions in the Republican Party (United States)#Conservatives|conservative]] or [[Factions in the Republican Party (United States)#Trumpists|MAGA]] wing of the Republican Party, Donalds was a member of the [[Tea Party movement]]. 

Donalds was elected to the Florida House of Representatives in [[2016 Florida House of Representatives election|2016]], representing the [[Florida's 80th House of Representatives district|80th district]] for two terms before being elected to Congress in [[2020 United States House of Representatives elections in Florida|2020]]. He was reelected in 2022 and 2024. During his second congressional term, he was a nominee for [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|Speaker of the House]] in the [[Voting_methods_in_deliberative_assemblies#United_States_House_of_Representatives|roll-call votes]] of [[January 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|January 2023]] and [[October 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|October 2023]]. Donalds became the Republican nominee for governor of Florida in August 2026, and faces [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee [[David Jolly]] in the general election. If elected, Donalds would be Florida's first [[List of minority governors and lieutenant governors in the United States|Black governor]] and the first Black governor elected in the [[Deep South]].{{Efn|The first elected Black governor was [[Douglas Wilder]] of Virginia. He was elected in 1989}}

== Early life and education ==
Byron Lowell Donalds was born and raised in the [[Crown Heights, Brooklyn|Crown Heights]] neighborhood in the New York City [[Boroughs of New York City|borough]] of [[Brooklyn]].<ref name="auto1">{{Cite web|url=https://www.news-press.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/09/25/u-s-house-district-19-byron-donalds-black-conservative-national-office/5793284002/|access-date=21 March 2024|title='Almost an oxymoron': Rising star Byron Donalds is a Black conservative aiming for national office|first=Amy Bennett|last=Williams|website=Fort Myers News-Press|date=September 25, 2020|archive-date=March 17, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240317181328/https://www.news-press.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/09/25/u-s-house-district-19-byron-donalds-black-conservative-national-office/5793284002/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|first=Stephanie|last=Akin|title=Byron Donalds leading in Florida GOP primary, raising party's diversity hopes|url=https://www.rollcall.com/2020/08/19/byron-donalds-leading-in-florida-gop-primary-raising-partys-diversity-hopes/|access-date=September 5, 2020|website=[[Roll Call]]|date=August 19, 2020|language=en|archive-date=September 3, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200903161254/https://www.rollcall.com/2020/08/19/byron-donalds-leading-in-florida-gop-primary-raising-partys-diversity-hopes/|url-status=live}}</ref> One of three children, he was raised by his single mother.<ref name="auto1"/> In 1996, Donalds graduated from [[Nazareth Regional High School (Brooklyn)|Nazareth Regional High School]] in [[East Flatbush, Brooklyn|East Flatbush]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nazareth Alumni Byron Donalds '96 Elected Florida State Representative |work=[[Xaverian Brothers]] Sponsored Schools |url=https://www.xbss.org/nazareth-alumni-byron-donalds-96-elected-florida-state-representative/|access-date=September 5, 2020|language=en-US|archive-date=January 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210119042152/https://xbss.org/nazareth-alumni-byron-donalds-96-elected-florida-state-representative/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Donalds is of Jamaican and Panamanian heritage.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Donalds |first=Byron |date=October 25, 2021 |title=RULE 11: HAVE A VISION |url=https://donalds.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=456 |access-date=June 28, 2024 |archive-date=June 28, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240628033925/https://donalds.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=456 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McLeod |first1=Sheri |title=Jamaican-American Rep. Byron Donalds announces bid for Florida governor |url=https://www.caribbeannationalweekly.com/community-news/jamaican-american-rep-byron-donalds-announces-bid-for-florida-governor/ |access-date=2025-08-15 |work=Caribbean National Weekly |date=2025-03-10 |archive-date=September 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250914154137/https://www.caribbeannationalweekly.com/community-news/jamaican-american-rep-byron-donalds-announces-bid-for-florida-governor/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 1997, Donalds was charged with [[marijuana]] possession, but the charges were dropped as part of a [[Diversion program|pre-trial diversion program]], and he was fined $150 ({{Inflation|US|150|1997|r=-1|fmt=eq}}).<ref name=Naples>{{cite news |last=Sarkissian |first=Arek |access-date=21 March 2024 |url=https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/08/05/lawyer-banker-compete-collier-hendry-house-race/88117996/ |title=Lawyer, banker compete in Collier, Hendry House race |work=[[Naples Daily News]] |date=August 5, 2016 |quote=Donalds ... faced a marijuana possession charge in Leon County in 1997, but he was given pre-trial diversion and he paid a $150 fine, court records show. |archive-date=March 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240319221911/https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/08/05/lawyer-banker-compete-collier-hendry-house-race/88117996/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=Wright>{{cite news |last=Wright |first=Warren |title=Tea Party star answers to charges of a criminal past |work=[[WFTX-TV|Fox 4 Now]] |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzPMeyw-urk |via=YouTube |date=April 28, 2014 |access-date=March 21, 2024 |quote=He claims he was arrested for possession of pot, not distribution, and the second offense was for theft, not taking a bribe. |archive-date=March 21, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240321162910/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzPMeyw-urk |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2000, he pleaded [[no contest]] to a felony theft charge for allegedly attempting to defraud a bank (by depositing a bad check), but his record was later sealed and [[Expungement_in_the_United_States#Florida|expunged]].<ref name=Naples /><ref name=Wright /><ref>{{cite news |last=Bridges |first=C. |url=https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/2023/01/04/byron-donalds-naples-florida-nominated-matt-gaetz-speaker-of-the-house/69778279007/ |title=One of the surprise nominees for Speaker of the House is a Florida rep. Who is Byron Donalds? |work=[[Naples Daily News]] |date=January 4, 2023 |access-date=21 March 2024 |quote=When Donalds was 18, after a party at his apartment, he was arrested for possession of marijuana. Two years later, a female acquaintance convinced him he could make a thousand dollars by depositing a bad check. |archive-date=March 20, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240320012705/https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/2023/01/04/byron-donalds-naples-florida-nominated-matt-gaetz-speaker-of-the-house/69778279007/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |first=Eugene |last=Scott |title=Who is Byron Donalds? The latest Republican nominee for House speaker. |language=en-US |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/01/04/house-speaker-mccarthy-donalds-gop/ |date=January 4, 2023 |access-date=January 5, 2023 |issn=0190-8286 |archive-date=January 5, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105172959/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/01/04/house-speaker-mccarthy-donalds-gop/ |url-status=live }}</ref>  According to an attorney consulted by the [[fact-checking]] site [[PolitiFact]], "Donalds would not have been able to get his record expunged if the state considered him a convicted felon."<ref>{{cite news |last=Sherman |first=Amy |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/aug/06/casey-askar/florida-gop-candidates-ad-misleads-about-rivals-cr/ |title=Florida GOP candidate's ad misleads about rival's criminal history |work=[[PolitiFact]] |date=August 6, 2020 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200810111114/https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/aug/06/casey-askar/florida-gop-candidates-ad-misleads-about-rivals-cr/ |archive-date=2020-08-10 |access-date=21 March 2024 }}</ref>

Donalds attended [[Florida A&M University]], subsequently transferring to [[Florida State University]]. In 2002, he graduated from FSU with a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in finance and marketing.<ref name="auto1"/><ref>{{Cite web|last=Buzzacco-Foerster|first=Jenna|date=August 4, 2016|title=Meet Byron Donalds, Republican running in House District 80|url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/218137-meet-byron-donalds-republican-running-house-district-80|access-date=September 5, 2020|website=[[Florida Politics]]|language=en-US|archive-date=September 9, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200909141812/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/218137-meet-byron-donalds-republican-running-house-district-80|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Biography {{!}} U.S. Congressman Byron Donalds |url=https://donalds.house.gov/about/ |access-date=2026-07-24 |website=donalds.house.gov |language=en |archive-date=August 20, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260820034902/https://donalds.house.gov/about/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Early career ===
[[File:The 2019 Second Step Presidential Justice Forum (48959680341).jpg|thumb|right|175px|Donalds and President [[Donald Trump]] in 2019 with a former inmate freed by the [[First Step Act]]]]
Donalds began his professional career in 2003 as a [[credit analyst]] at TIB Bank. He was promoted to senior credit analyst in 2004, and later promoted to [[Credit manager|commercial credit manager]], assistant vice president, and credit manager.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|title=Meet Byron| url= https://www.byrondonalds.com/about |access-date= September 5, 2020| website= Byron Donalds for Congress| language= en|archive-date= April 8, 2023| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230408173729/https://www.byrondonalds.com/about| url-status= live}}</ref> Donalds left TIB Bank in 2007 and took a position as a [[portfolio manager]] at CMG Surety LLC. In 2015, he joined [[Wells Fargo Advisors]] as a [[financial advisor]].<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://www.contactmypolitician.com/about-politician/5971/contact-byron-donalds| title= Contact Byron Donalds| website= www.contactmypolitician.com| access-date= January 7, 2023| archive-date= January 7, 2023| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230107013333/https://www.contactmypolitician.com/about-politician/5971/contact-byron-donalds|url-status=dead}}</ref> 

==Florida House of Representatives (2016–2020)==
In 2010, Donalds left the [[Florida Democratic Party|Democratic Party]] and joined the [[Republican Party of Florida|Republican Party]].<ref name="auto4">{{Cite web |title=Byron Donalds issues cease-and-desist letter to Casey Askar campaign |first=Jacob |last=Ogles |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/356605-byron-donalds-issues-cease-and-desist-letter-to-casey-askar-campaign/ |access-date=January 11, 2022 |website=[[Florida Politics]] |date=August 11, 2020 |language=en |archive-date=January 11, 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220111142349/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/356605-byron-donalds-issues-cease-and-desist-letter-to-casey-askar-campaign/ |url-status=live }}</ref> After becoming involved in the [[Tea Party movement]], he was encouraged to run for office.<ref name="auto1"/> Over time, he became part of the [[MAGA]] wing of the Republican Party.<ref>{{cite news |last=Broadwater |first=Luke |url= https://www.nytimes.com/live/2023/10/24/us/house-speaker-vote |title=House Speaker Election: Third Republican Nominee for Speaker Is Out |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=24 October 2023 |quote=favorites of the party's right wing. |archive-date=March 11, 2024 |access-date=March 11, 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240311001812/https://www.nytimes.com/live/2023/10/24/us/house-speaker-vote |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bunn |first=Curtis |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/byron-donalds-house-speaker-political-views-rcna64368 |title=Who is Byron Donalds? Rebel Republicans rally around the Florida congressman to defeat McCarthy |work=[[NBC News]] |date=5 January 2023 |quote=conservative Republican |archive-date=March 11, 2024 |access-date=March 11, 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240311001812/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/byron-donalds-house-speaker-political-views-rcna64368 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Fineout |first=Gary |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2023/07/27/desantis-florida-history-standards-donalds-00108628 |title=DeSantis to GOP Rep. Donalds: Whose side are you on? |work=[[Politico]] |date=27 August 2023 |quote=DeSantis lumped the conservative Black Republican... |archive-date=March 11, 2024 |access-date=March 11, 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240311001812/https://www.politico.com/news/2023/07/27/desantis-florida-history-standards-donalds-00108628 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-28 |title=Byron Donalds pitches himself as Trump's gov pick, DeSantis extension in kickoff rally |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/28/byron-donalds-kickoff-rally-trump-governor-pick-desantis-extension-00259216 |access-date=2025-08-18 |website=POLITICO |archive-date=July 21, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250721055237/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/28/byron-donalds-kickoff-rally-trump-governor-pick-desantis-extension-00259216 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In 2012, Donalds was a candidate for the U.S. House of Representatives in [[Florida's 19th congressional district]]. He finished fifth of six candidates.<ref name="auto3">{{Cite web| url= https://archive.naplesnews.com/news/politics/elections/byron-donalds-decides-not-to-run-for-congress-ep-312982098-341439621.html|title=Byron Donalds decides not to run for Congress|access-date=March 21, 2024|website=[[Naples News]]|date=February 11, 2014|archive-date=March 21, 2024|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240321224618/https://archive.naplesnews.com/news/politics/elections/byron-donalds-decides-not-to-run-for-congress-ep-312982098-341439621.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2014, he was reported as a likely candidate for the U.S. House in [[Florida's 19th congressional district]] after [[Trey Radel]] resigned, but did not run.<ref name="auto3"/>

Donalds was elected to the [[Florida House of Representatives]] for [[Florida's 80th House of Representatives district|District 80]] in [[2016 Florida House of Representatives election|2016]]. During his Florida House tenure, he chaired the Insurance and Banking Subcommittee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://nbc-2.com/election/2020/11/03/republican-byron-donalds-wins-district-19-congressional-seat/ |url-status=live |title= Republican Byron Donalds wins District-19 Congressional seat|date=November 4, 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201203020327/https://nbc-2.com/election/2020/11/03/republican-byron-donalds-wins-district-19-congressional-seat/ |archive-date=2020-12-03 |access-date=21 March 2024 |agency=Associated Press |work=[[WBBH-TV|NBC2]] }}</ref>

== U.S. House of Representatives (2021–present)==
=== Elections ===
==== 2020 ====
{{Main|2020 United States House of Representatives elections in Florida#District 19}}
[[File:Byron Donalds swearing in 2.jpg|thumb|224x224px|Donalds being ceremonially sworn in by House minority leader [[Kevin McCarthy]]]]
Donalds was the Republican nominee for [[Florida's 19th congressional district]] in the [[2020 United States House of Representatives elections in Florida|2020 election]], running to succeed retiring incumbent [[Francis Rooney]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Conradis |first=Brandon |date=November 3, 2020 |title=Republican Byron Donalds wins House race in Florida |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/524171-republican-byron-donalds-wins-house-race-in-florida |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=December 29, 2020 |archive-date=January 19, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119145423/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/524171-republican-byron-donalds-wins-house-race-in-florida/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="auto2">{{Cite web |title= Byron Donalds - 2018 - 2020 ( Speaker Oliva ) &#124; Florida House of Representatives |url= https://www.myfloridahouse.gov/Sections/Representatives/details.aspx?MemberId=4636 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170208174434/https://www.myfloridahouse.gov/Sections/Representatives/details.aspx?MemberId=4636 |url-status=live |archive-date=2017-02-08 |accessdate=January 29, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Black-American Members by Congress, 1870–Present |work=U.S. House of Representatives: History, Art & Archives |url=https://history.house.gov/Exhibitions-and-Publications/BAIC/Historical-Data/Black-American-Representatives-and-Senators-by-Congress/ |accessdate=February 2, 2021 |archive-date=February 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220223023801/https://history.house.gov/Exhibitions-and-Publications/BAIC/Historical-Data/Black-American-Representatives-and-Senators-by-Congress/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He won a crowded nine-way Republican primary by 770 votes over state representative [[Dane Eagle]], finishing just over the threshold to avoid a recount.<ref name=NewsPress>{{Cite web |last1=Bennett Williams |first1=Amy |title=Byron Donalds wins Republican primary election for U.S. Congressional District 19, will face Democrat Cindy Banyai |url=https://www.news-press.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/08/19/elections-2020-byron-donalds-to-face-cindy-banyai-in-congressional-district-19-race/3395873001/ |website=[[The News-Press]] |date= August 19, 2020 |access-date=August 21, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Ogles|first=Jacob|date=January 6, 2020| title= Byron Donalds announces bid for Francis Rooney's congressional seat| url= https://floridapolitics.com/archives/314948-byron-donalds-announces-hes-running-for-congress|access-date= September 5, 2020|website=[[Florida Politics]]|language=en-US|archive-date=September 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200913074407/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/314948-byron-donalds-announces-hes-running-for-congress|url-status=live}}</ref> Republicans have a 550,000-voter advantage over Democrats in registration, and [[Florida Gulf Coast University]] professor Peter Bergerson noted that the Republican primary is almost always the real contest for most races in the area.<ref name="NewsPress" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/florida-gop-voter-edge-continues-to-expand/ |title=Florida GOP voter edge continues to expand |website=[[CBS News]] |date=July 17, 2023 |access-date=21 March 2024 |archive-date= July 1, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250701034613/https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/florida-gop-voter-edge-continues-to-expand/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In August 2020, anonymous text messages were sent to constituents in the 19th district claiming that Donalds was dropping out of the race. Donalds later clarified via tweet that he was not dropping out and called the messages "illegal".<ref>{{Cite web|first1=Alex|last1=Marquardt|first2=Paul P.| last2= Murphy|title=Fake texts and YouTube video spread disinformation about Republican primary candidate on election day|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/18/politics/byron-donalds-fake-texts-florida-republican-primary/index.html| access-date=September 5, 2020|website=[[CNN]]|date=August 18, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|  date=August 18, 2020| title=Fake text claims Byron Donalds dropped out of Congressional race, he calls it illegal|url=https://nbc-2.com/news/politics/2020/08/18/fake-text-claims-byron-donalds-dropped-out-of-congressional-race-he-calls-it-illegal/|access-date=September 5, 2020|website=NBC2 News|language=en-US |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818204801/https://nbc-2.com/news/politics/2020/08/18/fake-text-claims-byron-donalds-dropped-out-of-congressional-race-he-calls-it-illegal/ |archive-date=2020-08-18 }}</ref>

During his campaign, Donalds called himself a "Trump-supporting, gun-owning, liberty-loving, pro-life, politically incorrect Black man."<ref>{{cite web| first1=Clare| last1=Foran| first2=Simone| last2=Pathe| title=4 things to watch in Tuesday's primaries in Florida and Wyoming| url= https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/18/politics/primary-elections-what-to-watch/index.html| website= [[CNN]]| date= August 18, 2020| access-date=January 4, 2023| archive-date=January 5, 2023| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230105050250/https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/18/politics/primary-elections-what-to-watch/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He stated his support for economic freedom, clean water, nuclear power, and decreased government involvement in healthcare. He opposed the [[Green New Deal]].<ref name="auto1"/>

In the November general election, Donalds defeated Democratic nominee Cindy Banyai. Donalds said he would focus on policy related to water quality in [[Southwest Florida]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Williams|first=Amy Bennett| title='Blessed and highly favored' Byron Donalds wins election for U.S. Congressional District 19, pledges water quality No. 1 priority| url=https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/03/us-congressional-house-district-19-election-results-winner-byron-donalds-cindy-banyai/6045674002/|date=November 3, 2020|access-date=November 6, 2020| website=[[Naples News]]|language=en-US|archive-date=November 6, 2020|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201106181354/https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/03/us-congressional-house-district-19-election-results-winner-byron-donalds-cindy-banyai/6045674002/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=August 30, 2016|title=Donalds, Rommel win state House seats| url=https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/08/30/donalds-rommel-win-state-house-seats/89595188/|first=Arek|last=Sarkissian|access-date=December 13, 2018|newspaper=[[Naples News]]|archive-date=January 19, 2023|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230119145421/https://www.naplesnews.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/08/30/donalds-rommel-win-state-house-seats/89595188/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Florida House of Representatives - Byron Donalds - 2016-2018| url= https://www.myfloridahouse.gov/Sections/Representatives/details.aspx?MemberId=4636&LegislativeTermId=87|access-date=December 13, 2018|website=Myfloridahouse.gov|archive-date=December 30, 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221230050356/https://www.myfloridahouse.gov/Sections/Representatives/details.aspx?MemberId=4636&LegislativeTermId=87|url-status=live}}</ref> Upon his swearing-in on January 3, 2021, Donalds became the sixth person to represent this district since its creation in 1983 (it had been the 13th District from 1983 to 1993, the 14th from 1993 to 2013, and has been the 19th since 2013).

=== Tenure in Congress ===
[[File:Congressman Byron Donalds with Miami-Dade County Sheriff Rosie Cordero-Stutz, January 2025.jpg|thumb|Donalds with [[Miami-Dade County]] [[County sheriff (Florida)|sheriff]] [[Rosie Cordero-Stutz]], 2025]]
In late 2020, Donalds was identified as a participant in the "Freedom Force", a group of incoming House Republicans who "say they’re fighting against [[socialism]] in America".<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://www.businessinsider.com/gop-house-members-freedom-force-counter-aoc-squad-2020-11|title=A group of incoming GOP House members, calling themselves the 'Freedom Force,' are trying to counter Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez's 'Squad'| first=Mia| last=Jankowicz| website=[[Business Insider]]| date=November 30, 2020| access-date= December 29, 2020| archive-date=January 21, 2021| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210121215528/https://www.businessinsider.com/gop-house-members-freedom-force-counter-aoc-squad-2020-11|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/republicans-freedom-force-new-york-california-reps-michelle-steel-nicole-malliotakis|title=GOP Congresswoman-elect on forming 'Freedom Force': Left is 'totally out of line' with mainstream|first=Caleb|last=Parke|date=December 1, 2020|website=[[Fox News]]|access-date=December 29, 2020| archive-date=December 10, 2020|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201210090248/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/republicans-freedom-force-new-york-california-reps-michelle-steel-nicole-malliotakis|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|first=Poppy|last=Noor |access-date=21 March 2024 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/30/republicans-freedom-force-squad-burgess-owens|title=The 'Freedom Force': Republican group takes on the Squad and 'evil' socialism|date=November 30, 2020|website=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> In 2021, Donalds was blocked from joining the [[Congressional Black Caucus]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Goba|first=Kadia|date=June 9, 2021|title=The Congressional Black Caucus Is Blocking A Black Republican From Joining The Group|url=https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/kadiagoba/byron-donalds-congressional-black-caucus-membership|url-status=live|access-date=June 12, 2021|website=[[BuzzFeed News]]| language=en|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210610011855/https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/kadiagoba/byron-donalds-congressional-black-caucus-membership |archive-date=June 10, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web| last=Lonas |first= Lexi| date=June 9, 2021| title=Congressional Black Caucus blocking Black House Republican from joining group |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/house/557691-congressional-black-caucus-blocking-black-house-republican-from-joining-group| url-status=live| access-date=June 12, 2021| website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|language=en|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210610012728/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/557691-congressional-black-caucus-blocking-black-house-republican-from-joining-group |archive-date=June 10, 2021}}</ref> [[File:U.S. Rep. Byron Donalds with U.S. Attorney General Pam Bondi and U.S. Secretary of Transportation Sean Duffy.jpg|thumb|Donalds with Attorney General [[Pam Bondi]] and Transportation Secretary [[Sean Duffy]], 2025]]

==== 2020 election ====
In January 2021, Donalds [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|voted to object]] to the certification of electors from Arizona and Pennsylvania in the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/politics/os-ne-scott-rubio-biden-certification-20210107-cqf3nlqro5hodj22e2xnfvgmuy-story.html?outputType=amp |title=Florida Sen. Rick Scott votes yes on objection to Biden's presidency; Rubio votes no |first=Mark |last=Skoneki |date=January 7, 2021 |website=[[The Orlando Sentinel]] |access-date=January 7, 2021 |archive-date=January 7, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210107182340/https://www.orlandosentinel.com/politics/os-ne-scott-rubio-biden-certification-20210107-cqf3nlqro5hodj22e2xnfvgmuy-story.html?outputType=amp |url-status=live }}</ref> Donalds later said that [[Joe Biden]] was not the legitimate president of the United States, a claim he repeated in a 2023 ''[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]'' interview.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |first=Pablo |last=Manriquez |title= Ron DeSantis May Have a Successor in the Wings: Byron Donalds |url= https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2023/07/ron-desantis-florida-successor-freedom-caucus-byron-donalds |website=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]] |date=July 13, 2023 |access-date=July 20, 2023 |language=English |archive-date=July 15, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230715214616/https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2023/07/ron-desantis-florida-successor-freedom-caucus-byron-donalds |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== January 2023 speaker of the House election ====
{{main|January 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election}}

On January 3, 2023, Donalds received one vote in the [[118th United States Congress|118th Congress]]'s first [[January 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|election]] for Speaker of the House, from [[Chip Roy]].<ref name="Texas Tribune">{{cite web| first=Matthew| last=Choi| url=https://www.texastribune.org/2023/01/03/chip-roy-us-house-speaker-kevin-mccarthy/| title=U.S. Rep. Chip Roy emerges as key GOP agitator in U.S. House speaker fight| newspaper=[[The Texas Tribune]]| date=January 3, 2023| access-date=January 4, 2023| archive-date=January 4, 2023| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230104181253/https://www.texastribune.org/2023/01/03/chip-roy-us-house-speaker-kevin-mccarthy/| url-status=live}}</ref> Donalds voted for [[Kevin McCarthy]] on the first two ballots and for [[Jim Jordan]] on the third.<ref>{{cite web| first=Julia| last=Mueller| url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3797231-20th-gop-opponent-to-mccarthy-explains-his-vote-for-jordan/| title=20th GOP opponent to McCarthy explains his vote for Jordan| newspaper=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]| date=January 3, 2023| access-date=January 4, 2023| archive-date=January 4, 2023| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230104180815/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3797231-20th-gop-opponent-to-mccarthy-explains-his-vote-for-jordan/| url-status=live}}</ref> On January 4, on the fourth ballot, Roy nominated Donalds for speaker, and he received 20 votes.<ref name="Texas Tribune"/> This marked the first time two Black lawmakers were nominated for speaker, the other being Democratic nominee [[Hakeem Jeffries]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://michiganchronicle.com/2023/01/05/for-first-time-in-history-two-black-lawmakers-nominated-as-house-speaker/|title=For First Time In History, Two Black Lawmakers Nominated As House Speaker &#124; The Michigan Chronicle|date=January 5, 2023|access-date=May 8, 2023|archive-date=May 8, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230508153349/https://michiganchronicle.com/2023/01/05/for-first-time-in-history-two-black-lawmakers-nominated-as-house-speaker/|url-status=live}}</ref> He was nominated by [[Lauren Boebert]] on the fifth ballot, and again received 20 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/colorado/news/colorado-congresswoman-lauren-boebert-nominates-byron-donalds-house-speaker/|agency=[[Associated Press]]|title=Colorado Congresswoman Lauren Boebert nominates Rep. Byron Donalds for House Speaker|website=[[CBS News]]|date=January 4, 2023|accessdate=January 4, 2023|archive-date= January 4, 2023|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230104203728/https://www.cbsnews.com/colorado/news/colorado-congresswoman-lauren-boebert-nominates-byron-donalds-house-speaker/|url-status=live}}</ref> He was sequentially nominated by [[Scott Perry]],<ref>{{cite news|first=Jonathan|last=Tamari|url=https://www.inquirer.com/politics/pennsylvania/pa-scott-perry-speech-nominates-byron-donalds-20230104.html|title='Washington is broken': Scott Perry nominated a GOP alternative for House speaker|newspaper=[[The Philadelphia Inquirer]]|date=January 4, 2023|accessdate=January 4, 2023|archive-date=January 4, 2023|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230104230046/https://www.inquirer.com/politics/pennsylvania/pa-scott-perry-speech-nominates-byron-donalds-20230104.html|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Dan Bishop]], [[Andy Biggs]], [[Matt Rosendale]], and [[Anna Paulina Luna]] on the sixth to tenth ballots. He was not nominated on the 11th ballot,<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sangal |first1=Aditi |last2=Vogt |first2=Adrienne |last3=Hayes |first3=Mike |last4=Chowdhury |first4=Maureen |last5=Hammond |first5=Elise |date=2023-01-05 |title=Live updates: House speaker vote and news |url=https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/house-speaker-leadership-vote-01-05-23/index.html |access-date=2023-01-05 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en |archive-date=January 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230106043952/https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/house-speaker-leadership-vote-01-05-23/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> but still received 12 votes. Donalds switched<ref>{{cite news|first=Jackson|last=Bakich|url=https://floridianpress.com/2023/01/breaking-donalds-paulina-luna-flip-vote-for-mccarthy/|title=BREAKING: Donalds, Paulina Luna Flip, Vote for McCarthy|newspaper=The Floridian|date=January 6, 2023|archive-date=January 7, 2023|access-date=January 7, 2023|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230107031631/https://floridianpress.com/2023/01/breaking-donalds-paulina-luna-flip-vote-for-mccarthy/|url-status=live}}</ref> his vote back to McCarthy after House Republican leadership made concessions to House Freedom Caucus members.
[[File:Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent with Congressman Byron Donalds in May 2025.jpg|thumb|Donalds with Treasury Secretary [[Scott Bessent]]]]
====Subcommittee hearing on Hurricane Ian====
{{main|United States Congress hearing on Hurricane Ian}}

On August 10, 2023, Donalds was one of the main questioners during the [[United States House Oversight Subcommittee on Government Operations|United States House Subcommittee on Government Operations and the Federal Workforce]]'s [[United States Congress hearing on Hurricane Ian|televised investigative hearing]] on the federal government's response to and overall recovery efforts from [[Hurricane Ian]] in 2022. Questions from Donalds led Congress to discover various government agencies had "regulatory hurdles, miscommunication, and burdensome administrative requirements", which made recovery efforts difficult, and that FEMA did not "tap into the expertise on the local level and pre-certify people from the city".<ref name="Hearing">{{cite web |author1=[[United States House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform]] |title="Weathering the Storm: Oversight of the Federal Response and Recovery Efforts in Southwestern Florida following Hurricane Ian" |url= https://www.congress.gov/event/118th-congress/house-event/116300 |publisher=United States Congress |access-date= 26 July 2025 |date=10 August 2023 |archive-date=August 22, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250822062417/https://www.congress.gov/event/118th-congress/house-event/116300 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="TheHill">{{cite web |last1=Kelly |first1=Caleigh |title=Lawmakers grill feds over delays in Hurricane Ian response |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4149077-lawmakers-grill-feds-over-delays-in-hurricane-ian-response/ |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=11 August 2023 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |archive-date=September 2, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250902212226/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4149077-lawmakers-grill-feds-over-delays-in-hurricane-ian-response/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Kruger |first1=Ryan |title=Congressional subcommittee asks what went wrong with Ian recovery |url=https://www.fox4now.com/news/local-news/congressional-subcommittee-asks-what-went-wrong-with-ian-recovery |website=[[WFTX-TV]] |access-date=6 August 2025 |date=10 August 2023 |archive-date=September 26, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250926011557/https://www.fox4now.com/news/local-news/congressional-subcommittee-asks-what-went-wrong-with-ian-recovery |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=FEMA Taken to The Woodshed |url=https://beachtalkradionews.com/fema-taken-to-the-woodshed/ |website=Beach Talk Radio News |date=10 August 2023 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |archive-date=August 6, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250806230144/https://beachtalkradionews.com/fema-taken-to-the-woodshed/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== October 2023 candidacy for speaker ====
{{main|October 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election}}

Donalds was a candidate for [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives]] in the [[October 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|October 2023 election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Perry |first=Mitch |date=2023-10-20 |title=Florida's Byron Donalds is now a candidate for U.S. House Speaker |url=https://floridaphoenix.com/2023/10/20/floridas-byron-donalds-is-now-a-candidate-for-u-s-house-speaker/ |access-date=2023-10-21 |website=Florida Phoenix |language=en-US |archive-date=October 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231021040350/https://floridaphoenix.com/2023/10/20/floridas-byron-donalds-is-now-a-candidate-for-u-s-house-speaker/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Dolan |first= Paul |date=2023-10-20 |title=Byron Donalds running for speaker of the House |url= https://winknews.com/2023/10/20/byron-donalds-speaker-of-the-house/ |access-date=2023-10-21 |website=WINK News |language=en-US |archive-date=October 21, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231021032633/https://winknews.com/2023/10/20/byron-donalds-speaker-of-the-house/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[File:President Donald Trump gives remarks after presenting the newly-created "Medal of Sacrifice" to three fallen officers' families from the Palm Beach County Sheriff's Office, Monday, May 19, 2025 P20250519JB-0227.jpg|thumb|Donalds speaking in the [[Oval Office]] during a ceremony rewarding the newly-created "[[Medal of Sacrifice]]" to three fallen deputies of the [[Palm Beach County Sheriff's Office]], May 2025]]

====Comments on Jim Crow era====
In a political outreach event in June 2024, Donalds said of the [[Jim Crow]] era:<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walsh |first1=Sean |title=Rep. Byron Donalds draws backlash for expressing nostalgia for Jim Crow era during Philly event |url=https://www.inquirer.com/politics/philadelphia/bryon-donalds-philadelphia-jim-crow-20240605.html |access-date=June 25, 2024 |work=[[The Philadelphia Inquirer]] |date=June 5, 2024 |archive-date=June 25, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240625002455/https://www.inquirer.com/politics/philadelphia/bryon-donalds-philadelphia-jim-crow-20240605.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>Phillip, Abby.  “[https://transcripts.cnn.com/show/cnap/date/2024-06-05/segment/01 GOP's Rep. Byron Donalds (R-FL) Says, Black Family Was Together In Jim Crow Era; Trump Racks Up Major Legal Wins Delaying Georgia And Florida Cases; Trump's Two Cases On Hold Until Post-Elections; WSJ Report Reveals Biden Showing Signs Of Slipping; Senate Blocks Bill On Right To Contraception Access. Aired 10-11p ET] {{Webarchive| url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240908081612/https://transcripts.cnn.com/show/cnap/date/2024-06-05/segment/01| date= September 8, 2024 }}”, [[CNN]] (5 Jun 2024).</ref>

<blockquote>You see, during Jim Crow, the black family was together. During Jim Crow, more black people were not just conservative—black people have always been conservative-minded—but more black people voted conservatively. And then, [[United States Department of Health and Human Services|HEW]], [[Lyndon Johnson]], and then you go down that road, and now we are where we are. What's happened in America the last ten years, and I say it because it's my contemporaries…you're starting to see more black people be married in homes raising kids.</blockquote>

After some backlash against those remarks, Donalds denied "that Black people were doing better under Jim Crow" or that "Jim Crow is great", and also denied having been inaccurate when he said that black marriage rates were relatively high during that era.<ref>Richards, Zoë. [https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-defends-comments-jim-crow-fiery-exchange-joy-reid-rcna155969 "Rep. Byron Donalds defends comments about Jim Crow"] {{Webarchive| url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250215063933/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-defends-comments-jim-crow-fiery-exchange-joy-reid-rcna155969 |date=February 15, 2025 }}, [[NBC News]] (6 Jun 2024).</ref>

In February 2024, [[Donald Trump]] said that Donalds was among those he was considering as a [[running mate]], and media outlets subsequently listed him as a [[2024 Republican Party vice presidential candidate selection|potential vice-presidential nominee]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Steck |first=Andrew Kaczynski, Em |date=2024-03-29 |title=Byron Donalds, potential VP pick, once attacked Trump and praised outsourcing, privatizing entitlements {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/03/29/politics/kfile-byron-donalds-trump-criticisms |access-date=2025-11-16 |website=CNN |language=en |archive-date=March 10, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260310134832/https://www.cnn.com/2024/03/29/politics/kfile-byron-donalds-trump-criticisms/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Rep. Byron Donalds weighs in on Trump potential VP 'short-list' |url=https://baynews9.com/fl/tampa/politics/2024/02/22/rep--donalds-weighs-in-on-trump-potential-vp--short-list- |access-date=2025-11-16 |website=baynews9.com |language=en |archive-date=June 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250619005817/https://baynews9.com/fl/tampa/politics/2024/02/22/rep--donalds-weighs-in-on-trump-potential-vp--short-list- |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== Voting Record ====
The Heritage Action Scorecard gives Donalds a 97% rating as a conservative [https://heritageaction.com/scorecard/members/D000032], compared to the 87% rating of average House Republicans. While in Congress he consistently cast votes in support of President Donald Trump and his political agenda.

* '''January 7, 2021''' - Voted in objection to certification of the Electoral College vote, hours after the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]] that sought to disrupt the certification proceedings.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202111}}</ref>

* '''January 13, 2021''' - A week after the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]] Donalds voted against the impeachment of Donald Trump for the high crime of Incitement of Insurrection.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202117}}</ref>

* '''July 3, 2025''' - Voted in Support of ''An Act to Provide for Reconciliation Pursuant to Title II of H. Con. Res. 14'' (dubbed by Trump the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act|One Big, Beautiful Bill Act]]). The comprehensive law contains hundreds of provisions enacting the tax and spending policies central to President Trump's second-term agenda.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2025190}}</ref>

* '''April  16, 2026''' - Voted against a bill that would reinstate and extend Temporary Protected Status (TPS) protections for Haitian immigrants<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026120}}</ref>. On July 27, 2026, TPS for Haitians nationwide was officially terminated under the direction of President Trump. According to [[Human Rights Watch]], before the termination, 330,000 Haitians were covered by TPS nationally, including 158,000 Haitians in Florida.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-07-02 |title=US: Haitians Set to Lose Protections, Risk Return to Violence {{!}} Human Rights Watch |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/07/02/us-haitians-set-to-lose-protections-risk-return-to-violence |access-date=2026-10-17 |language=en}}</ref>

* '''Repeatedly Blocked Attempts to Limit Trump's War Powers.''' Over a five-month span from April to July 2026, Donalds repeatedly voted against a monthly recurring House resolution to require the president to remove armed forces from hostilities with Iran.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026114}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026170}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026199}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026199}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026282}}</ref>

* '''June 9, 2026''' - Voted in support of the [[Secure America Act]],  a law that implements President Trump's core immigration policies. It appropriated approximately $70 billion in supplemental funding for border security, with increased funding for [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|ICE]] on top of the original funding in the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act|One Big Beautiful Bill Act.]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Clerk, US House of Representatives - Vote Detail |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026214}}</ref>

=== Committee assignments ===
For the [[119th United States Congress|119th Congress]], Donalds has been assigned on [[United States House Committee on Financial Services|Committee on Financial Services]], which includes [[United States House Financial Services Subcommittee on Digital Assets, Financial Technology, and Artificial Intelligence|Subcommittee on Digital Assets, Financial Technology, and Artificial Intelligence]] and [[United States House Financial Services Subcommittee on Financial Institutions|Subcommittee on Financial Institutions]], as well as [[United States House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform|Committee on Oversight and Government Reform]], which includes [[United States House Oversight Subcommittee on Economic Growth, Energy Policy, and Regulatory Affairs|Subcommittee on Economic Growth, Energy Policy, and Regulatory Affairs]] and [[United States House Oversight Subcommittee on Military and Foreign Affairs|Subcommittee on Military and Foreign Affairs]].<ref>{{cite web| title= List of Standing Committees and Select Committees of the House of Representatives| url= https://clerk.house.gov/committee_info/scsoal.pdf|publisher=Clerk of the United States House of Representatives |access-date= December 3, 2025|archive-date=April 28, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428030613/https://clerk.house.gov/committee_info/scsoal.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> He is a member of the [[Congressional Blockchain Caucus]],<ref>{{cite web| title=Members| url= https://congressionalblockchaincaucus-schweikert.house.gov/members| publisher=Congressional Blockchain Caucus| date= July 13, 2023| access-date=29 August 2024}}</ref> [[Freedom Caucus]],<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://floridianpress.com/2020/09/byron-donalds-says-hell-join-house-freedom-and-black-congressional-caucuses/ |title= Byron Donalds says he'll join House Freedom and Black Congressional Caucuses |date= September 18, 2020| access-date=June 10, 2024|archive-date=July 11, 2021|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210711055951/https://floridianpress.com/2020/09/byron-donalds-says-hell-join-house-freedom-and-black-congressional-caucuses/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Sharia-Free America Caucus]],<ref>{{Cite web| date=January 15, 2026|title=Congressman Self and Congressman Roy's Sharia Free America Caucus Surges in Congress, Adding Twenty-Four Members in Under One Month|url=https://keithself.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-self-and-congressman-roys-sharia-free-america-caucus-surges|access-date=August 4, 2026|website=Congressman Keith Self| language=en|archive-date=April 17, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260417144108/https://keithself.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-self-and-congressman-roys-sharia-free-america-caucus-surges|url-status=live}}</ref> and the [[Republican Study Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web| date=December 6, 2017| title=Membership| url=https://rsc-banks.house.gov/about/membership |access-date=March 28, 2021| website=Republican Study Committee |language=en |archive-date= April 6, 2021| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210406003657/https://rsc-banks.house.gov/about/membership |url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== CLC stock trades complaint ===
On September 5, 2024, the [[Campaign Legal Center]] (CLC) filed an ethics complaint accusing Donalds of violating the [[Stop Trading on Congressional Knowledge]] (STOCK) Act by failing to disclose over 100 stock trades, totaling up to $1.6 million, made between 2022 and 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-09-05 |title=Rep. Byron Donalds of Florida Fails to Disclose up to $1.6 Million in Stock Trades — Campaign Legal Center Files Ethics Complaint |url=https://campaignlegal.org/press-releases/rep-byron-donalds-florida-fails-disclose-16-million-stock-trades-campaign-legal |access-date=2026-05-02 |website=Campaign Legal Center |language=en |archive-date=May 27, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260527223601/https://campaignlegal.org/press-releases/rep-byron-donalds-florida-fails-disclose-16-million-stock-trades-campaign-legal |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Metzger |first=Bryan |title=Rep. Byron Donalds didn't properly disclose up to $1.6 million in stock trades, violating federal law |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/byron-donalds-stock-trades-insider-trading-ethics-complaint-2024-9 |access-date=2026-05-02 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US |archive-date=May 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260516005123/https://www.businessinsider.com/byron-donalds-stock-trades-insider-trading-ethics-complaint-2024-9 |url-status=live }}</ref>[[File:Senator Ashley Moody and Congressman Byron Donalds with NASA Chief of Staff Brian Hughes, 2025.jpg|thumb|Donalds with Senator [[Ashley Moody]] and [[NASA]] chief of staff Brian Hughes, 2025]][[File:U.S. Rep. Byron Donalds with U.S. Senator Rick Scott, 2025.jpg|thumb|Donalds with Senator [[Rick Scott]], 2025]]

== 2026 Florida gubernatorial campaign ==
In February 2025, Donalds announced his candidacy for [[governor of Florida]] in the [[2026 Florida gubernatorial election|2026 election]]. Days earlier, Trump had posted on [[Truth Social]] that Donalds would have his "Complete and Total Endorsement."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leonard |first1=Kimberly |last2=Matat |first2=Stephany |title=Rep. Byron Donalds, backed by Trump, says he's running for Florida governor |url= https://apnews.com/article/byron-donalds-florida-governor-elections-2026-trump-5884b17305d6ef64408934dbdd65a210 |access-date=February 25, 2025 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=February 25, 2025 |language=en |archive-date=February 26, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250226025939/https://apnews.com/article/byron-donalds-florida-governor-elections-2026-trump-5884b17305d6ef64408934dbdd65a210 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lesniewski |first=Niels |date=2025-02-25 |title=Byron Donalds, with Trump endorsement, announces bid for governor |url=https://rollcall.com/2025/02/25/byron-donalds-florida-governor-trump/ |access-date=2025-08-18 |website=Roll Call |language=en-US |archive-date=October 7, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20251007183942/https://rollcall.com/2025/02/25/byron-donalds-florida-governor-trump/ |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In September 2026, Donalds raised questions in the media when he removed the item "Enact the Trump agenda" from the top issues on his website. He subsequently reaffirmed his support, stating Trump's agenda is a "commonsense agenda" <ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-24 |title=Florida governor candidate Byron Donalds stands by Trump after some mentions of the president removed from website - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/florida-governor-candidate-byron-donalds-trump-mentions-disappear-from-website/ |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

If elected, Donalds would be Florida's first Black governor.

== Political positions==
===Agriculture===
In March 2024, Donalds was one of ten House Republicans who signed a letter to the [[United States House Committee on Agriculture|House Agriculture Committee]] opposing the inclusion of the Ending Agricultural Trade Suppression (EATS) Act or similar language in the 2024 [[United States farm bill|farm bill]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Rep. Anna Paulina Luna Leads in Letter Fighting Against Foreign Control of the U.S. Pork Industry |url=https://luna.house.gov/posts/icymi-rep-anna-paulina-luna-leads-in-letter-fighting-against-foreign-control-of-the-u-s-pork-industry |publisher=Anna Paulina Luna |access-date=26 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240414004611/https://luna.house.gov/posts/icymi-rep-anna-paulina-luna-leads-in-letter-fighting-against-foreign-control-of-the-u-s-pork-industry |archive-date=14 April 2024 |date=8 March 2024}}</ref> The EATS Act would have [[federal preemption|preempted]] state and local laws regulating agricultural products sold across state lines, including [[animal welfare]] laws like [[2018 California Proposition 12|California's Proposition 12]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Clayton |first1=Chris |title=GOP Conservatives Oppose EATS Act Over Chinese Influence in US Pork Industry |url=https://www.dtnpf.com/agriculture/web/ag/news/article/2024/03/08/gop-conservatives-oppose-eats-act-us |website=Progressive Farmer |publisher=DTN |access-date=26 May 2025 |date=8 March 2024 |archive-date=August 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260804045655/https://www.dtnpf.com/agriculture/web/ag/news/article/2024/03/08/gop-conservatives-oppose-eats-act-us |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Economic policy===
Donalds supports expanding development of [[Data center|data centers]] in Florida.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Morel |first=Laura C. |date=October 15, 2026 |title=Byron Donalds is pro–data centers. So is his stock portfolio. |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/10/byron-donalds-is-pro-data-centers-so-is-his-stock-portfolio/ |access-date=2026-10-16 |website=Mother Jones |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Education===
Donalds supports charter schools and [[school voucher]] programs. According to ''[[Mother Jones (magazine)|Mother Jones]]'', in 2017, Donalds sponsored legislation that allocated millions of dollars of Florida state funding towards management fees for charter school companies.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Butler|first=Kiera|date=August 10, 2023 |title= "Betsy DeVos Was a Disaster. I Think Erika Donalds Could Be Worse."| url= https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/08/erika-donalds-byron-charter-hillsdale-optimaed-betsy-devos-education-secretary-trump/|access-date=2025-05-16|website=Mother Jones|language=en|archive-date=August 10, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260810055306/https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/08/erika-donalds-byron-charter-hillsdale-optimaed-betsy-devos-education-secretary-trump/|url-status=live}}</ref> Donalds's wife, Erika, operates OptimaEd, a business operating multiple charter schools in Florida. During his tenure in the Florida legislature, Donalds co-sponsored a bill that allowed charter schools to collect additional funding via tax initiatives. He also established a state school voucher program.<ref name="NYTimes">{{Cite web| last1= Berzon| first1=Alexandra|last2=Bender|first2=Michael|date=May 27, 2024|title=Trump Elevates a Conservative 'Warrior' on Education|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/27/us/politics/trump-campaign-byron-donalds-florida.html |access-date=August 10, 2026|website=New York Times|language=en|archive-date=September 30, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250930181041/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/27/us/politics/trump-campaign-byron-donalds-florida.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Byron and Erika Donalds were founding members of Florida Citizens Alliance, an organization that has sought to [[Book censorship in the United States|remove from schools books]] that it believes indoctrinate students. The Donaldses are also affiliated with [[Moms for Liberty]], which has also advocated for book removals.<ref name= "NYTimes"></ref> In 2017, Donalds introduced legislation making it easier for Florida residents to challenge school library books and textbooks they consider inappropriate.<ref>{{Cite news |date=May 5, 2017 |title=Florida parents may get a bigger say over school textbooks |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/237552-florida-parents-may-get-bigger-say-school-textbooks/ |access-date=August 10, 2026 |work=Florida Politics |language=en |archive-date= August 22, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250822013022/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/237552-florida-parents-may-get-bigger-say-school-textbooks/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Environment===
Donalds [[Climate change denial|does not believe]] that [[climate change]] is caused by humans, saying that "the science is not settled on that".<ref>{{Cite web|last=So|first=Kat|date=December 18, 2025|title=Climate Deniers of the 119th Congress and the Second Trump Administration|url=https://www.americanprogress.org/article/climate-deniers-of-the-119th-congress-and-the-second-trump-administration/|access-date=2026-08-04|website=Center for American Progress|language=en-US|archive-date=July 29, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260729091138/https://www.americanprogress.org/article/climate-deniers-of-the-119th-congress-and-the-second-trump-administration/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=August 30, 2023 |title=Rep. Byron Donalds: 'I Don't Believe in Man-Made Climate Change' |url=https://grabien.com/story?id=437443 |access-date=2026-08-04 |work=Fox News |language=en-US |archive-date=August 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260804044019/https://grabien.com/story%3Fid%3D437443 |url-status=live }}</ref> Donalds blamed European insurance companies for inflating insurance costs for homeowners in Florida by factoring climate change into their risk analysis. He suggested that climate change risk should not matter in assessing insurance costs.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gancarski|first=A.G.|date=March 18, 2025|title=Byron Donalds questions whether 'climate change risk' should matter in reinsurance formula| url= https://floridapolitics.com/archives/726712-donalds-climate-change-risk/|access-date=2026-08-04|work=Florida Politics|language=en-US|archive-date=August 4, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260804044018/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/726712-donalds-climate-change-risk/|url-status=live}}</ref>   

===Foreign policy===
In 2023, ''[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]'' characterized Donalds as "part of a Republican minority wing that has consistently opposed [[Ukraine]] funding." Donalds opposed U.S. funding to defend Ukraine in the [[Russo-Ukrainian war]], citing the U.S. federal deficit as justification. "To be blunt, we're running a $2 trillion deficit. Any money we give to Ukraine, we're borrowing from our future." Donalds said.<ref>{{Cite news| last= Sforza|first=Lauren|date=September 19, 2023|title=Donalds ahead of Zelensky meetings: 'There's no money in the House' for Ukraine|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4212756-donalds-ahead-of-zelensky-meetings-theres-no-money-in-the-house-for-ukraine/|access-date=August 10, 2026|work=The Hill|language=en|archive-date=June 20, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240620024512/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4212756-donalds-ahead-of-zelensky-meetings-theres-no-money-in-the-house-for-ukraine/|url-status=live}}</ref> Donalds supports U.S. funding for [[Israel]] in the [[Gaza War]], stating "We should stand behind Israel 100%."<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Richards|first1=Zoe|last2=Obregon|first2=Natalie|date=April 21, 2025|title=Rep. Byron Donalds' town hall turns contentious over questions about DEI and Gaza|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-town-hall-turns-contentious-questions-dei-gaza-rcna202293|access-date=August 10, 2026|work=NBC News| language=en| archive-date=February 14, 2026| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260214093947/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-town-hall-turns-contentious-questions-dei-gaza-rcna202293|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|date=April 18, 2024| title=We need to take care of Israel: Rep. Byron Donalds| url= https://www.foxbusiness.com/video/6351166455112|access-date=August 10, 2026|work=Fox Business| language=en|archive-date=August 11, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260811040016/https://www.foxbusiness.com/video/6351166455112|url-status=live}}</ref> Donalds supported the Trump administration starting the [[2026 Iran War]]. He predicted a "succinct" resolution to the conflict, saying: "We don't do six-year engagements. We go over with our men and women, the best military in the world. We blow up all your stuff. And then we go home."<ref>{{Cite news| last1=Gancarski |first1= A.J.| date=March 30, 2026| title=Byron Donalds expects 'succinct' resolution to Iran conflict| url= https://floridapolitics.com/archives/787924-byron-donalds-succinct-iran/|access-date=August 10, 2026|work=Florida Politics|language=en|archive-date=April 7, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260407235302/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/787924-byron-donalds-succinct-iran/|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2026, Donalds defended the Trump administration's conduct in Iran, saying the US could not let Iran obtain a nuclear weapon and that Trump would be "derelict in his duty" if he acted otherwise.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Richards|first1=Zoe|last2=Obregon|first2=Natalie|date=April 21, 2025|title=Rep. Byron Donalds' town hall turns contentious over questions about DEI and Gaza| url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-town-hall-turns-contentious-questions-dei-gaza-rcna202293 |access-date= August 10, 2026| work=NBC News| language=en| archive-date=February 14, 2026| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260214093947/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/byron-donalds-town-hall-turns-contentious-questions-dei-gaza-rcna202293|url-status=live}}</ref> In an interview with ''[[Meet the Press]]'' in April 2026, Donalds said he supports "full-scale regime change" in [[Cuba]].<ref>{{Cite news |date= April 12, 2026 |title=Byron Donalds says he supports 'full-scale regime change' in Cuba: Full interview |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/byron-donalds-says-he-supports-full-scale-regime-change-in-cuba-full-interview-261198405575 |access-date=August 10, 2026 |work=[[NBC News]] |language=en |archive-date=April 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260420001647/https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/byron-donalds-says-he-supports-full-scale-regime-change-in-cuba-full-interview-261198405575 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Immigration===
In 2023, Donalds introduced the Reform Immigration Through Biometrics Act, which would require the collection of fingerprints, facial images, and iris scan for all travelers to the US. The legislation did not advance past committee. Donalds reintroduced the legislation in {{As of|2026|bare=yes}} as H.R. 8371.<ref>{{cite web|title=US Congressman Introduces Bill to Mandate Biometric Border Scanning|url=https://idtechwire.com/us-congressman-introduces-bill-to-mandate-biometric-border-scanning/|access-date=August 4, 2026|website=ID Tech Wire|date=April 23, 2026|archive-date=August 4, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260804045642/https://idtechwire.com/us-congressman-introduces-bill-to-mandate-biometric-border-scanning/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-04-21 |title=H.R.8371 - 119th Congress (2025-2026): Reform Immigration Through Biometrics Act |url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/8371 |access-date=2026-08-14 |website=www.congress.gov}}</ref>

On May 5, 2025, Donalds appeared on ''[[Meet the Press]]'' with [[Kristen Welker]] and said, "When it comes to due process, that is a privilege reserved for American citizens."<ref>{{cite news |title=Rep. Byron Donalds: 'It is not physically possible' to hire enough judges to process immigrants |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/rep-byron-donalds-it-is-not-physically-possible-to-hire-enough-judges-to-process-immigrants-238945349614 |access-date=May 15, 2025 |website=NBC News |date=May 5, 2025 |archive-date=May 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515063339/https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/rep-byron-donalds-it-is-not-physically-possible-to-hire-enough-judges-to-process-immigrants-238945349614 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Donalds opposes the "current interpretation" of [[birthright citizenship in the United States]]. He has said that birthright citizenship "has been abused beyond recognition." When the [[Supreme Court of the United States]] invalidated President Trump's executive order limiting birthright citizenship in ''[[Trump v. Barbara]]'', Donalds called the ruling "a terrible decision."<ref>{{cite web| last=Perry| first=Mitch| title=Birthright citizenship ruling by U.S. Supreme Court splits Florida lawmakers |url= https://floridaphoenix.com/2026/06/30/birthright-citizenship-ruling-by-u-s-supreme-court-splits-florida-lawmakers/ |access-date=August 4, 2026|website=Florida Phoenix|date=June 30, 2026|archive-date=August 4, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260804045708/https://floridaphoenix.com/2026/06/30/birthright-citizenship-ruling-by-u-s-supreme-court-splits-florida-lawmakers/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| last=Fishman| first=Taylor| title=Byron Donalds outlines Florida vision as governor's race begins to take shape| url= https://abc3340.com/news/nation-world/byron-donalds-outlines-florida-vision-as-governors-race-begins-to-take-shape-president-trump-endorsement-gubernatorial-bid-ron-desantis-republican-democrat-affordability-housing-taxes-education-immigration-sunshine-state-birthright-citizenship|access-date=August 4, 2026|website=ABC News|date=June 16, 2026|archive-date=August 4, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260804045642/https://abc3340.com/news/nation-world/byron-donalds-outlines-florida-vision-as-governors-race-begins-to-take-shape-president-trump-endorsement-gubernatorial-bid-ron-desantis-republican-democrat-affordability-housing-taxes-education-immigration-sunshine-state-birthright-citizenship|url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2026, Donalds visited the Venezuelan restaurant El Arepazo, in [[Doral, Florida|Doral]], [[Miami-Dade County]].<ref name= "Univision">{{cite web| title= Byron Donalds visitó El Arepazo en Doral: "Si tu TPS terminó, vamos a deportarte"| url= https://www.univision.com/local/miami-wltv/byron-donalds-campana-gobernador-contra-tps| date= 27 July 2026| website= [[TelevisaUnivision]]}}</ref> A journalist asked Donalds whether he would support legislation that offered a pathway to [[I-551|permanent residency]] for [[Venezuelan diaspora|Venezuelan immigrants]] previously eligible for [[Temporary Protected Status]].<ref name="Univision" /> Donalds said that any immigrant whose Temporary Protected Status ended would be [[Deportation in the second Trump administration|deported]] without a chance at another immigration status.<ref name="Univision" />

===Islam===
In January 2026, Donalds was named a founding member of the [[Sharia-Free America Caucus]], which seeks to [[Ban on sharia law|ban Sharia law]]. He said, "Sharia has no place in America. We will not surrender our freedoms."<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 15, 2026 |title=Congressman Self and Congressman Roy's Sharia Free America Caucus Surges in Congress, Adding Twenty-Four Members in Under One Month |url=https://keithself.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-self-and-congressman-roys-sharia-free-america-caucus-surges |website=Representative Keith Self |access-date=August 4, 2026 |archive-date=April 17, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260417144108/https://keithself.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-self-and-congressman-roys-sharia-free-america-caucus-surges |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Council on American–Islamic Relations]] (CAIR), a civil rights organization, named Donalds and other House members in a statement designating the Sharia Free America Caucus an anti-Muslim [[hate group]] and saying the caucus's agenda "would effectively ban the practice of the world's second largest religion in the United States."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sharia Free America Congressional Caucus Anti-Muslim Extremist Designation |url=https://www.cair.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Sharia-Free-Caucus-Designation-February-2026-1.pdf |website=Council on American-Islamic Relations |access-date=August 14, 2026 |archive-date=April 27, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260427175741/https://www.cair.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Sharia-Free-Caucus-Designation-February-2026-1.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

===LGBTQ rights===
In 2020, Donalds and six other Republicans in the Florida legislature introduced several anti-LGBTQ bills that would repeal local ordinances that protect LGBTQ employees, legalize [[conversion therapy]], and imprison doctors who provide transition-related medical care to minors.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 16, 2020|title=Florida Republicans submit 4 last-minute anti-LGBTQ bills ahead of 2020 legislative deadline| url= https://abcnews.com/Politics/florida-republicans-submit-minute-anti-lgbtq-bills-ahead/story?id=68316012| access-date= August 10, 2026| website=ABC News| language=en| archive-date=May 22, 2026| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260522144137/https://abcnews.com/Politics/florida-republicans-submit-minute-anti-lgbtq-bills-ahead/story?id=68316012|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2021, Donalds spoke at an event hosted by the Truth and Liberty Coalition, which has described homosexuality as "Satan's work". The organization was honoring Donalds's support for charter schools. At a [[Conservative Political Action Conference]] event, Donalds described heterosexual relationships as "the natural order that keeps society progressing."<ref name="NYTimes"></ref>

===Veterans===
Donalds voted against the [[Honoring our PACT Act of 2022]], which expanded [[United States Department of Veterans Affairs|VA]] benefits to veterans exposed to toxic chemicals during their military service.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Derby |first=Kevin |date=2022-06-09 |title=Marco Rubio, Brian Mast Help Shape Burn Pit Legislation |url=https://floridadaily.com/marco-rubio-brian-mast-help-shape-burn-pit-legislation/ |access-date=2025-05-21 |website=Florida Daily |language=en-US |archive-date=August 5, 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250805151822/https://floridadaily.com/marco-rubio-brian-mast-help-shape-burn-pit-legislation/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

